<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<subsection xml:id="objectives"><title>Objectives</title>
	<p>
		The syllabus of record for MTH<nbsp />123 states:
	</p>
        <blockquote><p>After successful completion of the course, students will be able to</p>
        <p>
<ol>
	<li><p>Explain the definition of the circular functions.                         </p></li>
	<li><p>Compute the circular functions exactly for common functions.              </p></li>
	<li><p>Graph sinusoidal functions and determine their equations.                 </p></li>
	<li><p>Explain the definition of the inverse trigonometric functions.            </p></li>
	<li><p>Perform computations of inverse trigonometric functions.                  </p></li>
	<li><p>Apply trigonometric identities to computations of trigonometric functions.</p></li>
	<li><p>Solve equations involving trigonometric functions.                        </p></li>
	<li><p>Solve application problems using trigonometric functions.                 </p></li>
</ol>
        </p>
</blockquote>
<p>Furthermore,
	Grand Valley State University's mission statement is: <q>At Grand Valley State University, we empower learners in their pursuits, professions, and purpose. The university enriches society through excellent teaching, active scholarship, advancement of equity, and public service.
</q></p>
<p>
This course is a step along the way to you achieving your own professional and personal goals.  In light of this, some of my goals for this course are for you 
</p>
<p>
<ul>
	<li><p>   to develop the skill of thinking mathematically,</p></li>
	<li><p>   to have a stronger idea of how trigonometry relates to future classes and to the physical world, and</p></li>
	<li><p>   to have an idea of what mathematics actually is.</p></li>
</ul>
</p>
	</subsection>
